</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Effect of Speech Tokens</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compared the results of using <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unit</em> and <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unit language</em> as speech tokens in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2 Acoustic-Semantic Gap ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ EchoX: Towards Mitigating Acoustic-Semantic Gap via Echo Training for Speech-to-Speech LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. It can be observed that using unit language achieves nearly twice the compression ratio while delivering superior performance. Additionally, we further compared the quality of the generated audio and found that both methods perform similarly in terms of audio quality, as shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.2 Acoustic-Semantic Gap ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ EchoX: Towards Mitigating Acoustic-Semantic Gap via Echo Training for Speech-to-Speech LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. However, the recognition accuracy of audio generated with unit language is significantly higher than that generated with units. This also indirectly indicates that when the model predicts speech tokens based on hidden representations, it is prone to error accumulation, leading to an increased error rate in the final model predictions.</p>
</div>
